---
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import ForFramework from '@/components/ForFramework.astro';
import Code from '@/components/Code.astro';
import MotionStage from '@/components/motion/MotionStage.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';
import MotionOptions from '@/components/motion/MotionOptions.astro';
import MotionTokens from '@/components/motion/MotionTokens.astro';
import Springs from '@/components/motion/scenes/Springs.astro';
import { SpringsControls } from '@/components/motion/scenes/SpringsControls';

const stylesheetCss = `@import '@udixio/motion/motion.css';`;

const stylesheetJs = `import '@udixio/motion/motion.css';`;

const springCss = `.card {
  transition-duration: var(--udx-motion-spring-fast-spatial-duration);
  transition-timing-function: var(--udx-motion-spring-fast-spatial-easing);
}`;

const durationCss = `.banner {
  transition-duration: calc(var(--udx-motion-duration-medium2) * var(--udx-motion-scale));
  transition-timing-function: var(--udx-motion-easing-standard);
}`;

const tokensTs = `import { defineTokens, resolveSpring } from '@udixio/motion';

// The expressive scheme is the default: 'standard' selects the other one.
const tokens = defineTokens({ scheme: 'standard' });
tokens.spring.fast.spatial.dampingRatio; // the physical parameters
tokens.distance.md; // 24
tokens.stagger.normal; // 50

// The linear() curve where the backend supports it, the fallback otherwise.
const timing = resolveSpring('spring.fast.spatial', { support: { linear: true, fallback: true } });`;
---
<MotionPage
  slug="tokens"
  title="Motion: springs and tokens"
  description="The CSS and TypeScript motion tokens of @udixio/motion: springs, durations, easings, distances and staggers, and the document scale."
  frameworkSwitch
>
  <MotionStage scene="springs" label="Live example: Springs and tokens" height={[712, 1024]}>
    <Fragment slot="controls"><SpringsControls client:visible size="page" /></Fragment>
    <Springs size="page" />
  </MotionStage>

  <p>
    A token names a timing: a spring, such as <code>spring.default.spatial</code>, or a duration, such as <code>duration.medium2</code>.
    Naming a token animates nothing. A motion, a component or your own transition uses it. The six balls above are one spring each:
    Send moves them to the far end of their lanes, and Play curves runs the curves below.
  </p>

  <h2>Import the stylesheet</h2>
  <p>
    You need the stylesheet when CSS reads the tokens: your own transitions that use the <code>--udx-motion-*</code>
    variables, the Udixio components that read <code>--udx-motion-scale</code>, and the route rules of
    <a href="/motion/page-transitions">page transitions</a>. The motions you define play without it: the runtime holds the
    same tokens in JavaScript, and applies the reduced-motion policy itself.
  </p>
  <p>Import it once, in the document stylesheet, outside shadow roots:</p>
  <Code data-code={stylesheetCss} data-language="css" />
  <p>Or, from the main module of your application:</p>
  <Code data-code={stylesheetJs} data-language="ts" />
  <p>
    The stylesheet is static, and it works in server-rendered HTML without JavaScript. It starts with the official cubic-bezier
    fallbacks, and switches to the generated <code>linear()</code> curves where the browser accepts that syntax. Keep the sibling CSS
    files it imports together when you serve or copy the package assets.
  </p>

  <h2>Name a token in a motion</h2>
  <p>
    In the definition of a motion, you name its timing in one of two ways. Use a spring token, such as
    <code>timing: 'spring.fast.spatial'</code>. Or use a duration token and an easing token, such as
    <code>timing: &#123; duration: 'duration.medium2', easing: 'easing.standard' &#125;</code>.
  </p>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">
      Put <code>data-motion="panel-in"</code> on the element, and call <code>connectMotion()</code> once for the page, after the
      definition.
    </p>
    <p slot="react">
      Render the element as a <code>&lt;Motion name="panel-in"&gt;</code> inside a <code>&lt;Presence&gt;</code>.
    </p>
    <p slot="angular">Put <code>udxMotion="panel-in"</code> on the element, and show it with <code>&#64;if</code>.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">
      Attach <code>&#123;&#64;attach motion('panel-in')&#125;</code> to the element, and add <code>transition:presence</code> inside an
      <code>&#123;#if&#125;</code> block.
    </p>
  </ForFramework>
  <p>The example defines two motions: <code>panel-in</code> takes a spring, and <code>banner-in</code> takes a curve.</p>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-tokens" live={false} />

  <h2>Options</h2>
  <p>Every verb takes a timing, except <code>scrub</code>, which follows the scroll. A loop takes a curve only.</p>
  <MotionOptions
    rows={[
      { name: 'timing', values: 'spring.* · { duration, easing }', default: "the verb's own", description: 'The timing of every field of the motion. Without it, the verb’s own timing applies: its default spring, or the curve of a loop.' },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Choose a spring</h2>
  <p>
    The stylesheet exposes six spring pairs: <code>--udx-motion-spring-&#123;fast,default,slow&#125;-&#123;spatial,effects&#125;-&#123;duration,easing&#125;</code>.
    The document defaults to the expressive scheme. A transition uses the duration and the easing of a pair:
  </p>
  <Code data-code={springCss} data-language="css" />
  <MotionTokens tokens={['spring.fast.spatial', 'spring.fast.effects', 'spring.default.spatial', 'spring.default.effects', 'spring.slow.spatial', 'spring.slow.effects']} />
  <p>
    The stylesheet sets the scheme of the document: <code>data-motion-scheme="standard"</code> on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> selects the
    standard one. The scheme of the stylesheet and the scheme that you pass to <code>defineTokens()</code> are independent: set both
    when you use both APIs.
  </p>

  <h3>Which spring to use</h3>
  <p>
    In the expressive scheme, the fast spatial spring overshoots its target by about 9.5 %, the default and slow spatial springs by
    about 1.5 %, and the effects springs not at all. In the standard scheme, the spatial springs overshoot by about 0.15 %. Use the fast
    spatial spring only on transforms of small pieces: a switch thumb, a small scale, the items of a FAB menu. Do not use it on layout
    edges with clipped content (height or width), on corner radii, or on an indicator that travels more than about 50 px: the overshoot
    reads as a glitch there.
  </p>

  <h2>Use a duration and an easing</h2>
  <p>
    The stylesheet names the durations from <code>--udx-motion-duration-short1</code> to <code>--udx-motion-duration-extra-long4</code>,
    and the easings <code>--udx-motion-easing-linear</code>, <code>--udx-motion-easing-standard</code>,
    <code>--udx-motion-easing-standard-accelerate</code> and <code>--udx-motion-easing-standard-decelerate</code>. A stylesheet uses them
    with the document scale, as in this rule:
  </p>
  <Code data-code={durationCss} data-language="css" />
  <MotionTokens tokens={['duration.short4', 'duration.medium2', 'duration.extraLong1', 'easing.linear', 'easing.standard', 'easing.standardDecelerate']} />
  <p>
    In TypeScript, <code>defineTokens()</code> returns the durations in milliseconds, and the easings as CSS strings.
    <code>resolveCurve(&#123; duration: 'duration.medium2', easing: 'easing.standard' &#125;)</code> returns both, for a curve timing.
  </p>

  <h2>Use a distance or a stagger</h2>
  <p>
    The distances are <code>--udx-motion-distance-sm</code>, <code>-md</code> and <code>-lg</code>: 8, 24 and 64 CSS pixels. The staggers
    are <code>--udx-motion-stagger-tight</code>, <code>-normal</code>, <code>-loose</code> and <code>-max</code>: 20, 50, 100 and 400
    milliseconds. <code>stagger-max</code> caps the start delay of each element of a cascade, not the end time of its last animation.
  </p>
  <p>These values are provisional, with low confidence. They need visual calibration, and they do not come from M3.</p>
  <MotionTokens tokens={['distance.sm', 'distance.md', 'distance.lg', 'stagger.tight', 'stagger.normal', 'stagger.loose', 'stagger.max']} />

  <h2>Scale the motion of the document</h2>
  <p>
    <code>--udx-motion-scale</code> is an inherited number: 1 by default, and 0 when <code>prefers-reduced-motion: reduce</code> is active,
    or when <code>data-motion-policy="reduce"</code> is set on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>. The value <code>auto</code>, an absent attribute
    and an unknown value follow the system preference.
  </p>
  <p>
    A motion multiplies the distances of its keyframes, and its durations, by the scale. A layout flight keeps its real geometry, and a
    gesture keeps its own release. A scale of 0 is not a reduced render by itself: each verb applies its own, see
    <a href="/motion/accessibility">Reduced motion</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Search component of Udixio has its own outline transition. It works without the stylesheet, with a 200 ms duration and a fixed
    easing, and the stylesheet provides its tokens.
  </p>

  <h2>Read the tokens in TypeScript</h2>
  <p>The TypeScript tokens are experimental: their shape may change while the shared motion model is designed.</p>
  <Code data-code={tokensTs} data-language="ts" />
  <p>
    <code>resolveSpring()</code> resolves a spring for the backend that plays it. It picks the <code>linear()</code> curve where the
    backend supports it, and the fallback otherwise.
  </p>

  <h2>Read the tokens from the manifest</h2>
  <p>
    The machine-readable manifest lists every token with its value, and the CSS variable each one is written as:
    <a href="/motion-manifest.json"><code>motion-manifest.json</code></a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Reduced motion</h2>
  <p>
    The tokens do not disable animation: apply the policy before you use a value, see <a href="/motion/accessibility">Reduced motion</a>.
    A number from <code>createSpring()</code>, in <code>@udixio/motion/animate</code>, reaches its target at once under reduced motion.
    That is why the balls above jump to their far end under the policy.
  </p>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If the browser does not accept <code>linear()</code>, the spring pairs use the cubic-bezier fallback. The fallback only approximates a spring: check the motion in the browsers you support.</li>
    <li>If you animate with a backend other than CSS, check its support yourself. The stylesheet detects <code>linear()</code> for the CSS syntax only, not for the rendering of every backend.</li>
    <li>If you use a distance or a stagger token, suppress its translation or its stagger under reduced motion yourself. The tokens do not do it.</li>
    <li>If you need final distances or staggers, calibrate them. They are provisional, with low confidence.</li>
  </ul>
</MotionPage>
